Adding "tagged unions" feature

```onyx
UnionExample :: union {
    Variant1 : str;
    Variant2 : i32;
}

v := UnionExample.{ Variant1 = "Union Example" };
switch v {
    case .Variant1 => str_value {
        printf("The string is: {}\n", str_value);
    }
    // ...
}
```

With this change I was able to run the updater on WSLv2 - guessing it should now work in Github Actions as well.

I noticed when I browsed to the old `iam_url` in chrome and opened the inspector, I couldn't interactively find the CSRF token at either of the two locations (`app.orcaCsrf.token` or `window.Csrf.fromCookie(null)`). So I changed it to a URL for building an inline IAM policy and suddenly a few things happened.

1) I could pull the CSRF token from the Chrome inspector.
2) When running the phantom code interactively, the callback on the `page.includeJs` started working. (Before it would print `Successfully logged in` and then just stop.

```
            console.log('Successfully logged in')
            page.includeJs(
                "https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.1.0/jquery.min.js",
                function() {
                    console.log("Successfully loaded jQuery");
                    page.evaluate(advisor);
                    console.log("Advisor started");
                }
            );
            console.log("Waiting for advisor to finish");
```

FYI for future debugging purposes the relevant github action is supplied with an access key and secret key to an IAM User. This IAM User has zero permissions. There's also an IAM Role in the same account with a trust policy allowing the IAM User to assume into it. The IAM Role also has zero permissions. So running the updater locally I set the following environment variables:

```
export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=blah
export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=blah
export AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-west-2
export AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=blah
export AWS_ROLE_NAME=the role NAME that the USER can assume into
```

