Create some tooling around `status_t` (see #17605):

- [x] record all creation of `status_t` in the code and put that in an ELF section
- [x] create some tooling that can read this data and make things with it:
  - [x] list all records in an ELF file
  - [x] decode a status
  - [x] report conflicts between module IDs

Open questions:

- with this infrastructure, we could have much more precise error tracing by replacing module_id/arg by just one number, unique to each particular executable. At the moment, `arg` is somewhat overloaded and can be a line or something else. This would require to change the `status_t` layout though.
- the crypto library has its own implementation of `status_t` with hardening, do we want to touch that? See #18673
 
**Rationale behind records:** using some compiler builtin, we detect whether the arguments to the macro are compile-time constants or not. By recording this information, and since most creation of status will only use constants, this means that we should be able to detect whether a particular value of `status_t` is uniquely decodable or not (and be able to uniquely decode most of them).

